#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054499 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the graph of the straight line below?\n\nI. $y=2x-1$\n\nII. $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x-1$\n\nIII. $2x-y=1$\n\n![](images/93/93dc9d447c15e4597549996b1f0b533cf86783b5cc5df032a4c46d4ca8934abc.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, we can choose $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,-1)$ and $(2,3)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/a9/a9f18453dedb1763908825301f80ef86307d3a3aceaf678bbe8579a42a0996f5.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y=2x-1.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-1)$ and $B(2,3),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1) & =2(0)-1 \\\\ -1 & =-1\\,✓ \\\\ (3) & =2(2)-1 \\\\ 3 & =3\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=2x-1$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x-1.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-1)$ and $B(2,3),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1) & =\\frac{1}{2}(0)-1 \\\\ -1 & =-1\\,✓ \\\\ (3) & =\\frac{1}{2}(2)-1 \\\\ 3 & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x-1$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $2x-y=1.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-1)$ and $B(2,3),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)-(-1) & =1 \\\\ 1 & =1\\,✓ \\\\ 2(2)-(3) & =1 \\\\ 1 & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2x-y=1$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that represented the line in the graph were $y=2x-1$ and $2x-y=1,$ corresponding to answer choices I and III. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054503 :question/problem "Given ${x}^{2} + {y}^{2} = \\sin   ( y ) ,$ find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y = y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( {x}^{2} + {y}^{2} ) &  = \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( \\sin   ( y ) ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( {x}^{2} ) + \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( {y}^{2} ) &  = \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( \\sin   ( y ) ) \\\\ 2 x + 2 y \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} &  = \\cos   ( y ) \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}2 x + 2 y \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} &  = \\cos   ( y ) \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} \\\\ 2 y \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} - \\cos   ( y ) \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} &  = - 2 x \\\\ ( 2 y - \\cos   ( y ) ) \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} &  = - 2 x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} &  = - \\frac{2 x}{2 y - \\cos   ( y )} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} &  = \\frac{2 x}{\\cos   ( y ) - 2 y}\\end{array}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054504 :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)$ for the curve $xy+y^2=2x^2+4.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y = y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+4) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4x \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4x \\\\ (x+2y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4x-y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4x-y}{x+2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at the point $\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm\n&=\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right)_{\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)}\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\left(\\dfrac{4x-y}{x+2y}\\right)_{\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)}\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{4\\cdot 1-2}{1+2\\cdot 2}\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{2}{5} \\, .\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054505 :question/problem "The equation $x^2-2x+y^2+4y=5$ represents a circle. The normal line at the point $\\left(0, -5 \\right)$ on the circle intersects the $x$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\n![](images/b6/b6c52a76654a4a545d356890d4deb04c74301e4091422a51e84551aeb3ec1152.png)" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(0,-5).$ Remember that the slope of the normal line, $m',$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\nm' = -\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y = y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (x^2-2x+ y^2+4y)\n\t&=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (5)\\\\[5pt]\t\n\t\n\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (x^2)-\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (2x)+\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (y^2)+\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (4y)&=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (5)\\\\[5pt]\t\n\t\n\t2x-2+2y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}+4\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}&=0\\\\[5pt]\t\n\t\n\t\n\t(2y+4)\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}&=2-2x\\\\[5pt]\t\n\t\n\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\t&=\\dfrac{2-2x}{2y+4}\n\t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\tm\n\t&=\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right)_{\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)}\\\\[5pt]\n\t&=\\left(\\dfrac{2-2x}{2y+4}\\right)_{\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)}\\\\[5pt]\n\t&=\\dfrac{2-2\\cdot 0}{2\\cdot (-5)+4}\\\\[5pt]\n\t&=\\dfrac{2}{-6}\\\\[5pt]\n\t&=-\\dfrac{1}{3} \\, .\n\t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is $m'=-\\dfrac{1}{m}=3.$ We can write the equation of the normal at the point $\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\ty-y_1 &=m'\\left(x-x_1 \\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\ty+5 &=3\\left(x-0 \\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\ty&=3x-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $x$-axis. At the point $P$ the $y$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $y=0$ and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\ty&=3x-5\\\\[5pt]\n\t0&=3x-5\\\\[5pt]\n\tx&=\\dfrac{5}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(\\dfrac{5}{3},\\,0 \\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054571 :question/problem "![](images/f8/f87e0eb91b6ec47ca8a4d64c2824396e613123ed37862975b606608ff70445f0.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=5-2x.$" :question/worked-solution "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(0,5), B(2,1)$ and $C(3,0).$ \n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(0,5)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5-2x \\\\ (5) & =5-2(0) \\\\ 5 & =5\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line:\n\n- Substituting the point $B(2,1)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5-2x \\\\ (1) & =5-2(2) \\\\ 1 & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does lie on the line:\n\n- Substituting the point $C(3,0)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5-2x \\\\ (0) & =5-2(3) \\\\ 0 & =-1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only points $A$ and $B$ lie on the line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054572 :question/problem "Given $xy+y^2=x^2,$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y = y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ (x+2y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x-y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x-y}{x+2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054602 :question/problem "Use the table below to plot the graph of $y=3x-2.$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td>$0$</td>\n<td>$1$</td>\n<td>$2$</td>\n<td>$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" :question/worked-solution "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=3x-2.$\nTherefore,\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=3(0)-2=-2,$\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=3(1)-2=1,$\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=3(2)-2=4,$ and\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=3(3)-2=7.$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td>$0$</td>\n<td>$1$</td>\n<td>$2$</td>\n<td>$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td>$-2$</td>\n<td>$1$</td>\n<td>$4$</td>\n<td>$7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/09/09e670353b2114b229769e2bd6b3eb4a9895389b818fc3f4ace6997a3e7efa3f.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of $y=3x-2.$\n\n![](images/d2/d2d8891102ce99020f544bd8d82a5ff0454080474ce4267ef5285db92f5b2ca0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054603 :question/problem "Sketch the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+3.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\nIt is often easiest to pick one of the points to have an $x$-coordinate of $x=0.$ So, for the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+3 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0)+3 \\\\  & =0+3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,3).$\n\nFor the second point, let's choose $x=4.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+3 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(4)+3 \\\\  & =-2+3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(4,1).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+3.$\n\n![](images/36/36689081acaacb24a09826c3927388697e77d92caeca900d053d33d0f99d2b8c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055013 :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(\\sqrt{2}, 1)$ for the curve $x^{2} + 4y^{2} = 6$." :question/worked-solution "Implicit differentiation gives $2x+8y\\,y'=0$, so $y'=-x/(4y)$ wherever $y\\ne0$. At $(\\sqrt2,1)$, the slope is $$-\\frac{\\sqrt2}{4}=-\\frac1{2\\sqrt2}.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055014 :question/problem "![](images/aa/aab03da9e57863ce49011b6e6d9e1cd2cb64aa4c3eabfe5f323f505074c3b987.png)\n\nThe equation $x^{2} + 4y^{2} = 6$ represents an ellipse. The normal line at the point $(\\sqrt{2}, 1)$ on the ellipse intersects the $x$-axis at the point $P$. What are the coordinates of $P$?" :question/worked-solution "Implicit differentiation gives the tangent slope $-x/(4y)=-1/(2\\sqrt2)$ at $(\\sqrt2,1)$. The normal slope is its negative reciprocal, $2\\sqrt2$. Thus the normal is $y-1=2\\sqrt2(x-\\sqrt2)$. Setting $y=0$ yields $$x=\\sqrt2-\\frac1{2\\sqrt2}=\\frac3{2\\sqrt2}.$$ Therefore $P=(\\tfrac3{2\\sqrt2},0)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055017 :question/problem "![](images/4a/4a5fd15c2282e11fda4e98f64557d0b69d79bbcdc26946a3f494841233c680be.png)\n\nThe equation $3x^{2} - 2y^{2} = 4$ represents a hyperbola. The normal line at the point $(2, 2)$ on the hyperbola intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P$. What are the coordinates of $P$?" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating gives $6x-4y\\,y'=0$, hence the tangent slope at $(2,2)$ is $3/2$. The normal slope is $-2/3$, so its equation is $y-2=-\\tfrac23(x-2)$. At $x=0$, $y=2+\\tfrac43=\\tfrac{10}3$. Thus $P=(0,\\tfrac{10}3)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055125 :question/problem "![](images/c6/c63af2b4cd9209077baee74e5f4f4059bc1c8c7e4b2cdc620c872d025fe5a4e9.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y = x + 1$.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The plotted points are A=(2,7), B=(4,5), C=(7,2). Only B satisfies y=x+1."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055138 :question/problem "Given that $x^{3} + y^{3} = 1$, calculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate implicitly: $3x^2+3y^2y'=0$. Hence $$\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x^2}{y^2}$$ wherever the curve has a finite derivative, namely $y\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055139 :question/problem "Given that $\\cos y + 2x^{2} - y = 10x$, calculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate implicitly: $$-\\sin y\\,y'+4x-y'=10.$$ Solving for the derivative gives $$\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{4x-10}{1+\\sin y}=\\frac{2(2x-5)}{\\sin y+1},$$ where the denominator is nonzero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055140 :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ for $xy + y^{2} = 3x^{2}$." :question/worked-solution "Apply the product and chain rules: $y+xy'+2yy'=6x$. Thus $$(x+2y)y'=6x-y\\implies\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{6x-y}{x+2y},$$ where $x+2y\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055141 :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)=2x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y) & =2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x) \\\\ [(x)(\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{dy}}{\\text{d}x})+(1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y))] & =2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{y}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y) & =2 \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) & =2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y(2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y))}{x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055296 :question/problem "![](images/20/20e05c0af32d6fe2643af2912d51ef1b3994374260ffaa4fc22002def474c15f.png)\n\nIdentify the points on the graph that lie on the line $y = 8x - 3$.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A=(-6,5), B=(1,5), C=(6,5). Only B satisfies y=8x-3."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055366 :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(0,\\,1)$ for the curve ${x}^{2}+xy-{y}^{2}=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+xy-{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2x+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)-2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2x+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y-2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ (2x+y)+(x-2y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2x+y}{x-2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(0,\\,1)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(0,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={\\left(-\\frac{2x+y}{x-2y}\\right)}_{(0,\\,1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\cdot 0+1}{0-2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{0+1}{0-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055368 :question/problem "![](images/4c/4ce176ba6bb73da8d6af410bedad4733cf3e705c70e28f133d771b89f78130c5.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The line crosses the y-axis at 2 and passes through (4,4); its slope is 1/2. Therefore y=x/2+2, statement $y = \\frac{1}{2}x + 2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055369 :question/problem "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y = 2x - 4$?" :question/worked-solution "The line has slope $2$ and vertical intercept $-4$. It passes through $(0,-4)$ and $(2,0)$, so choose the graph with these intercepts and positive slope $2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055370 :question/problem "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=x-2.$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td>$0$</td>\n<td>$1$</td>\n<td>$2$</td>\n<td>$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" :question/worked-solution "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=x-2.$ Therefore,\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=(0)-2=-2,$\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=(1)-2=-1,$\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=(2)-2=0,$ and\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=(3)-2=1.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nWe plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/4f/4fc0e4386dd956c2b98c844941ce2e27cd1c9ca8578aca4cf5142e6971911831.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of $y=x-2.$\n\n![](images/89/893dba533e0afffe05f9603293355ccf38b2723fcd91608ea03fe321e26a0d5f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055429 :question/problem "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y =-x + 1$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td>$0$</td>\n<td>$1$</td>\n<td>$2$</td>\n<td>$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=0,1,2,3$ into $y=-x+1$ gives $y=1,0,-1,-2$. The required graph passes through these four points and slopes downward."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055430 :question/problem "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y = \\frac{1}{2}x - 2$?" :question/worked-solution "The equation $y=\\frac12x-2$ has vertical intercept $-2$ and horizontal intercept $4$. Choose the line through $(0,-2)$ and $(4,0)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055431 :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d78eaaedd26a27d9163fdcddfce5c5ed429e441a75d361f0840935fce9235c6.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The line has intercepts (0,4) and (4,0), so its equation is y=4-x, statement $y = 4 - x$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059385 :question/problem "Which inequality corresponds to the interval $x \\in (1, 5)?$" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur interval contains only parentheses. So, the endpoints are *not* included. \n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{red}{(}}1, 5{\\color{red}{)}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our interval can be expressed as the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1 \\,\\,{\\color{red}{<      }}\\,\\,      x  \\,\\,{\\color{red}{<      }}\\,\\,      5\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059386 :question/problem "Write the interval $y\\in\\big(-4, \\sqrt{2}\\big]$ as an inequality." :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0}\\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given interval \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{{\\big(}}} -4, \\sqrt2\\, {\\color{blue}{\\big]}}\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $-4$ is *not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{red}{\\lt}}\\,.$\n\n- The right endpoint $\\sqrt2$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{blue}{\\leq      }}\\,.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality that corresponds to the given interval is \n\n$$\n-4 \\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\lt  }}\\,\\,   y \\,\\,{\\color{blue}{\\leq}}\\,\\, \\sqrt 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059387 :question/problem "What interval corresponds to the segment shown below?\n\n![](images/43/430481f9d0863b0bb5602f949e6f994d8007ea29fdd741a485d880df8d5d2dec.png)" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nThe given interval can be represented by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-2\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\leq}}  \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{red}{\\lt}} \\,\\, 2\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{blue}{[}}-2,2{\\color{red}{)}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059388 :question/problem "Express the inequality $1 \\le x \\le4$ in interval notation." :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints $1$ and $4$ *are* included. \n\n$$\n1\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{[}}1,4{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [1,4].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059452 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\left| {\\begin{array}{ccc}0 & - 3 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 8 \\\\ - 6 & - 12 & 1\\end{array}}\n\\right|.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 8 \\\\ -12 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-(-3)\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+6\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -12\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0(8+96)+3(8+48)+6(-96+48) \\\\  & =0+3\\cdot 56+6\\cdot (-48) \\\\  & =168-288 \\\\  & =-120\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059453 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $A$ given below?\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$\n\n- $A$ is **not** a singular matrix\n\n- $A$ is a singular matrix\n\n$$\nA={\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 7\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(0-4)-2(7-3)+3(4-0) \\\\  & =-4-2\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-4-8+12 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(A)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059454 :question/problem "Given that the determinant of  $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ k & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ is $2,$ find the value of the constant $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ k & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & =3\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}k & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}k & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(12-10)-1(3k-0)+2(2k-0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 3k+2\\cdot 2k \\\\  & =6-3k+4k \\\\  & =k+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $2,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k+6 & =2 \\\\ k & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059469 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\sin^4{\\theta}+\\sin^2{\\theta}\\cos^2{\\theta}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor $\\sin^2\\theta$ from the expression:\n\n$$\n\\sin^4{\\theta}+\\sin^2{\\theta}\\cos^2{\\theta} = \\sin^2{\\theta}\\cdot(\\sin^2{\\theta}+\\cos^2{\\theta})\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta = 1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot ({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}) & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin^4{\\theta}+\\sin^2{\\theta}\\cos^2{\\theta} = \\sin^2\\theta.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059470 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^3 \\theta}{\\sin\\theta}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^3 \\theta} {\\sin \\theta}\t=\t\\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\cdot (\\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^2 \\theta)} {\\sin \\theta}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot ({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (1)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^3 \\theta} {\\sin \\theta} = \\cot \\theta.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059471 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\dfrac{\\sin{\\theta}}{1+\\cos{\\theta}}+\\cot{\\theta}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the second term using the identity $\\cot \\theta = \\dfrac{\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sin{\\theta}}{1+\\cos{\\theta}}+\\dfrac{\\cos{\\theta}}{\\sin{\\theta}}\n$$\n\nNext, we put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} &= \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} &= \\frac{({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} \\\\ &= \\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ &= \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sin{\\theta}}{1+\\cos{\\theta}}+\\cot{\\theta} = \\csc\\theta.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059486 :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\sin x(2\\cos x - \\sin x) - \\cos^2x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 x$ and $\\cos^2 x$ terms. \n\n$$\n2\\sin x\\cos x -(\\sin^2x + \\cos^2 x)\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2{x} + \\cos^2{x} = 1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n2\\sin x\\cos x - 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin x(2\\cos x - \\sin x) - \\cos^2x = 2\\sin x\\cos x - 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059523 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}$ if $\\mathbf{a}=-4\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=12\\mathbf{i}$." :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}$, we just need to compute all of the $\\mathbf{i}$'s and $\\mathbf{j}$'s, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na - 2b &= (-4i + 6j) - 2(12i) \\\\\n&=-4i + 6j - 24i \\\\\n&= (-4i - 24i) + 6j \\\\\n&=-28i + 6j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059524 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -7, 15 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 14, -11 \\rangle$. Find $\\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{b}$." :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{b}$, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na - 3b &= ⟨ - 7, 15⟩ - 3 \\cdot ⟨14,-11⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 7, 15⟩ - ⟨42,-33⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 7 - 42, 15 - (-33)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 49, 48⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059525 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 5\\end{array} \\right]},$ $\\mathbf{b}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]},$ and $\\mathbf{c}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 3 \\\\ 7\\end{array} \\right]}.$ Find $2\\mathbf{a}-(\\mathbf{b}-5\\mathbf{c})$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2a - (b - 5c) &= 2 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} - (\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} - 5 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix} - (\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 35\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ -31\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 21\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059526 :question/problem "The vectors $\\vec{a}$ and $\\vec{b}$ are given in the picture below. Find the coordinates of $\\vec{a}+6\\vec{b}$.\n\n![](images/56/56b10e759084da6fb21c8421bc9ff6d74aac2602df28f6c8e478bd874d7d6e9f.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the picture, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\vec{a}=\\langle -3,2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\vec{b}=\\langle 4,5 \\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, to compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\vec{a} + 6\\vec{b} &= ⟨ - 3, 2⟩ + 6 \\cdot ⟨4, 5⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 3, 2⟩ + ⟨6 \\cdot 4, 6 \\cdot 5⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 3, 2⟩ + ⟨24, 30⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨21, 32⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059527 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -4, 6 \\rangle$. Find $\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid$." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid a \\mid &= \\sqrt{a_{x}^{2} + a_{y}^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-4)^{2} + (6)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16 + 36} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{13}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
